== HYPOTHESIS ==
Hypothesis (data-grounded): In NLSY79, a one-wave behavioural proxy for lower time preference "Was more money put into or taken out of R/spouse savings since last interview, 1989" predicts a LOWER BMI in 1992, i.e. the reverse-direction statement of the question also holds: more impatience goes with higher BMI, but the effect is small and much of it is not causal-looking. Crude: respondents who put money INTO savings had BMI 1992 -0.74 BMI units lower than the reference group "no change" (95% CI [-1.082, -0.393], p=0.000) and those with no savings +0.57 units higher (p=0.002); obesity prevalence fell monotonically from 22.4% (no savings) to 14.0% (put money in). With the pre-specified adjustment set (sex, race cohort, age bands, baseline BMI 1989, ASVAB, maternal & paternal grades, family size) the saver contrast shrinks to -0.25 BMI units (95% CI [-0.55, 0.05], p=0.11; joint Wald on the three categories F=2.20, p=0.09), while the ordinal patience index keeps the predicted sign: -0.097 BMI units per one step MORE patience (95% CI [-0.177, -0.017], p=0.018) - equivalently +0.10 BMI units (~0.018 SD) per step LESS patience - and the saver-vs-nonsaver impatience contrast is +0.121 (95% CI [0.015, 0.228], p=0.026). On the obesity margin the same contrast is stronger: saver odds ratio 0.74 (95% CI [0.583, 0.939], p=0.013) for BMI>=30 in 1992. Robustness: estimates are stable across median-impute+indicators (n=8503), complete case (n=6578) and MICE m=10; dropping the "no savings" category, log-BMI, age-linear/quadratic, and adding income/wealth all leave the same sign. But the effect is fragile to the baseline-outcome control: without BMI 1989 it is -0.53 (p=0.002), whereas the change-score specification (BMI 1992 minus BMI 1989, n=8448) gives +0.02 (95% CI [-0.385, 0.423], p=0.93) - i.e. essentially no forward effect. Heterogeneity: concentrated in women (-0.32) vs men (-0.08) and in the younger cohort, age 1979 14-18 (-0.44) vs 19-22 (+0.12); age x exposure interaction p=0.09. Verdict: SUPPORTS a positive impatience-BMI association only as a weak, partly confounded proxy effect - not as a demonstrated causal effect of time preference on adiposity.

